After a weeklong run of screening Christopher Nolan 's &#x201C; The Odyssey ,&#x201D; Universal saved the best for last, bringing out Steven Spielberg to moderate a one-on-one Q&A.

As the film ended and the house lights went up, the pair walked out to a standing ovation from an audience of about 600 guild members and voters who had come out to see the film &#x2014; many on repeat viewings.

Before Spielberg had a moment to praise Nolan for the $1.8 billion blockbuster, it was Nolan who stepped in and humbly told Spielberg, &#x201C;I just have to take a minute, because I've known you a long time, but I've never done a Q&A with you. When I was a younger man and a dear boy watching your movies, I don't think I ever really envisioned this moment where you would be asking questions about my film.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories ‘Avengers: Doomsday’ Including the Original X-Men ‘Just Felt Right,’ Says Kevin Feige: It's a 'Fun Way to Culminate' 25 Years Oscars: Best Original Score — Could One of Hollywood’s Most Overlooked Composers Finally Get His Moment?

Spielberg told Nolan he had seen the film in Imax and called it &#x201C;a showstopper.&#x201D;

When asked why now, and why he had chosen to adapt Homer's poem, Nolan answered, &#x201C;I always find it hard to figure out what to do next. Probably one of the reasons, if it's been unpredictable, as you say, it's because it's always a surprise to me as well. I do one project at a time. I'm not very good at developing multiple things at the same time, so I finish one film and then I look for what might be next.&#x201D;

He continued, &#x201C;What hadn't really been done in the modern era was Greek mythology and I think the reason is because it hadn't been done definitively. Part of the reason was because of the mythological elements; the visual effects techniques weren't really available in the 1930s or the 1950s. When the great eras of the classical world, the world of antiquity, was big in Hollywood. 'The Odyssey' hadn't been done on this scale with this movie budget, with the resources of modern technology: shooting on Imax, using visual effects and all the rest.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Nolan also talked about building his cast and choosing Matt Damon to play Odysseus. &#x201C;This is one of the greatest things Matt has ever done as an actor,&#x201D; Spielberg said.

Nolan said, &#x201C;Matt occurred to me right away because I've done two films with him in small parts. So I know from my own experience on the floor how incredible he is as an actor and how great he is to work with. How he's just an incredible team member. He's an incredible creative force. He's not just thinking about his own part. He's thinking about the whole narrative, which is a great asset. But also, he's grown up on camera. You know, from that kid in 'Saving Private Ryan.' He has this ability to open up a story and take us on a journey, in empathetic terms; that's incredibly powerful.&#x201D;

Spielberg took a moment to tell Nolan that one of his favorite scenes in the film featured Samantha Morton as the witchy goddess Circe, and he wanted to know how Nolan pulled it off. Spielberg said, &#x201C;She was wonderful &#x2026; That's one of my favorite scenes in the whole movie, where they all turn into pigs.&#x201D;

Nolan explained he didn't want to shoot in front of a green screen or use tracking markers. &#x201C;I wanted to be able to define the process.&#x201D; He said he watched transformation movies such as &#x201C;An American Werewolf in London&#x201D; and even Neil Jordan's &#x201C;A Company of Wolves&#x201D; for inspiration.

He revealed it was a collaboration with both the visual effects and special effects departments as they navigated the challenge. &#x201C;They came up with these heads that have a skull underneath that's fractured, but it's held together with magnets and then it's covered in a particular type of silicone skin that looked exactly wonderfully real, but was very flexible. So we built these heads of the guys, and each take, she could go around the table and do whatever she wanted and manipulate these guys however she wanted in the most horrible ways.&#x201D;

&#x201C;The Odyssey&#x201D; opened in July to a $264.1 million global debut, the biggest of Nolan&#x2019;s career. It has since crossed $1.8 billion, becoming his highest-grossing film. Universal and Nolan&#x2019;s team are continuing to focus on the format. Nearly every Los Angeles screening is tagged Imax 70mm, 70mm or 35mm, reminding viewers that the film was shot entirely using Imax cameras and built to be judged on a screen the size of a building and not on a laptop or their home TV using the Academy digital portal.
